WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:07.503
Every year, small birds called Arctic terns accomplish the amazing feat of crossing the globe from north to south.

2
00:00:07.512 --> 00:00:16.575
Every year, small eel larvae leave their birthplace and swim two thousand kilometers, even though they are only 1.5 centimeters long!

3
00:00:16.576 --> 00:00:23.274
Animal migration is the movement of organisms from one area to another and usually back again.

4
00:00:23.275 --> 00:00:27.341
Many animals migrate, but on different time scales.

5
00:00:27.342 --> 00:00:33.275
Migration may happen over a few months, as in the spring and fall migration of many birds.

6
00:00:33.276 --> 00:00:37.558
Or, it may take a lifetime, as in the migration of salmon.

7
00:00:37.559 --> 00:00:43.382
As adults, these fish return to the same streams where they were hatched in order to spawn.

8
00:00:43.383 --> 00:00:47.768
The many mysteries of migration have long baffled scientists.

9
00:00:47.769 --> 00:00:52.025
But the answers to some of the most puzzling questions are known.

10
00:00:52.026 --> 00:00:56.148
Most animals are happy to live their lives in the same place.

11
00:00:56.149 --> 00:01:02.819
Why do some of them embark on long and dangerous journeys? The first reason is changes in the weather.

12
00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:08.193
In the warmer seasons, food is usually plentiful, whereas in winter, food is scarce.

13
00:01:08.194 --> 00:01:14.025
So, in order to find enough food to survive, some animals must move to warmer climates.

14
00:01:14.026 --> 00:01:17.287
The second reason animals migrate is to breed.

15
00:01:17.288 --> 00:01:26.847
Migrating animals usually return to the same spot where their ancestors reproduced because it is safe and has enough food for them to raise their own offspring.

16
00:01:26.848 --> 00:01:29.850
How do animals know when it is time to leave?

17
00:01:29.851 --> 00:01:34.498
Both external and internal signals tell an animal when to migrate.

18
00:01:34.499 --> 00:01:46.813
External changes, like a scarcity of food or colder weather, can start migration, and internal signals, caused by certain hormones, can also tell an animal when it's time to go.

19
00:01:46.814 --> 00:01:54.375
Once they have started on their journeys, animals use a variety of special abilities to guide them to their destinations.

20
00:01:54.376 --> 00:01:59.811
At the simplest level, animals rely on the currents of the wind or ocean to guide them.

21
00:01:59.812 --> 00:02:06.406
For example, sparrows in eastern North America catch winds that carry them to South America.

22
00:02:06.407 --> 00:02:12.991
Other animals use familiar landmarks, like coastlines, mountain ranges, and rivers, to navigate.

23
00:02:12.992 --> 00:02:17.185
Animals may also rely on the sun and the stars for direction.

24
00:02:17.186 --> 00:02:28.340
The way the sun's rays refract in the water helps fish to swim in the right direction, while birds that migrate at night use the position of the stars to guide their journeys.

25
00:02:28.341 --> 00:02:35.340
Some birds, notably pigeons, have the ability to find their destination even if they are taken far off course.

26
00:02:35.341 --> 00:02:46.495
Scientists have discovered tiny crystals of magnetite, a magnetic material, in their brains, and they believe these crystals might help them use the earth's magnetic field as a guide.

27
00:02:46.496 --> 00:02:52.223
Most species, however, use a combination of these navigation techniques when they migrate.

28
00:02:52.224 --> 00:03:01.590
For some animals, like monarch butterflies, migration routes are innate, meaning that the butterflies are born already knowing the path to take.

29
00:03:01.591 --> 00:03:14.238
Every fall, when the weather gets colder, adult monarchs leave the Rocky Mountains in the United States and Canada and migrate to central Mexico, as far as 2,900 kilometers away.

30
00:03:14.239 --> 00:03:19.967
The butterflies fly at such high altitudes that they are invisible to the human eye.

31
00:03:19.968 --> 00:03:27.975
When they arrive in Mexico, groups of butterflies congregate in the same place, and trees may be completely covered with them.

32
00:03:27.976 --> 00:03:35.389
As the winter draws to a close, the butterflies mate and then fly north again, laying eggs along the way.

33
00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:42.300
The eggs hatch, grow, and then travel northward, following the same migration path as their parents.

34
00:03:42.301 --> 00:03:48.163
Many other animals, like Arctic terns, must teach their young their migratory routes.

35
00:03:48.164 --> 00:04:00.659
The Arctic tern is the most famous migratory bird because it travels halfway around the world and back, approximately 22,000 kilometers every year─the longest distance for any bird.

36
00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:06.485
It migrates from its breeding grounds in the Arctic to the other side of the world, Antarctica.

37
00:04:06.486 --> 00:04:10.434
Arctic terns are social birds that live in large groups.

38
00:04:10.435 --> 00:04:18.474
Right before migration, the noisy terns suddenly get quiet, and then they all take off and begin their three-month journey.

39
00:04:18.475 --> 00:04:24.417
Along the way, terns sustain themselves by swooping into the ocean to catch fish to eat.

40
00:04:24.418 --> 00:04:30.089
Young Arctic terns migrate south with their parents, but are left behind in the spring.

41
00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:35.285
They live in Antarctica for two years, and then, they return to the Arctic on their own.

42
00:04:35.286 --> 00:04:39.171
They learned the way from their parents and will teach it to their young.

43
00:04:39.172 --> 00:04:44.166
Scientists believe that some species navigate by using their sense of smell.

44
00:04:44.167 --> 00:04:56.775
For example, certain types of Pacific and Atlantic salmon return to the exact spot in the river where they were born by somehow sniffing it out, even after spending almost their entire lives in the ocean.

45
00:04:56.776 --> 00:05:04.276
They make this long journey because female salmon must migrate back to their birthplace before they release their eggs.

46
00:05:04.277 --> 00:05:09.934
Some salmon migrate hundreds, even thousands, of kilometers to reach their breeding grounds.

47
00:05:09.935 --> 00:05:17.425
After spawning, the female salmon usually die in the same stream in which they were born─a true cycle of life.

48
00:05:17.426 --> 00:05:29.978
The salmon's annual return to certain rivers is so reliable that many Native Americans on the western coasts of America and Canada hold a ceremony to honor the salmon when they return each year.

49
00:05:29.979 --> 00:05:38.298
These people catch the salmon for food, so for them, these migratory fish are a symbol of Nature's ability to provide.

50
00:05:38.299 --> 00:05:41.972
Other fish use currents to direct them during their migration.

51
00:05:41.973 --> 00:05:49.124
European eels actually start their lives as larvae in the Sargasso Sea, in the western part of the Atlantic Ocean.

52
00:05:49.125 --> 00:05:58.905
Despite being only 1.5 centimeters long, the larvae are carried all the way to Europe on the Gulf Stream, a current that flows across the Atlantic.

53
00:05:58.906 --> 00:06:03.109
This journey takes anywhere from seven months to three years.

54
00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:16.170
To reach freshwater rivers inland, the young eels overcome all sorts of challenges: they dig through wet sand, slide through wet grass, and even pile their bodies on top of each other to climb over obstacles!

55
00:06:16.171 --> 00:06:23.107
Once in freshwater, the male and female eels live up to twenty years and fifty years, respectively.

56
00:06:23.108 --> 00:06:28.985
When the adult eels are ready to breed, they must make the long journey back to the Sargasso Sea.

57
00:06:28.986 --> 00:06:32.251
Eels change their bodies especially for this trip.

58
00:06:32.252 --> 00:06:43.810
Before an adult eel begins to migrate, its eyes grow to allow it to see in the dim light of the ocean, and its body turns a silver color that makes it nearly invisible to predators.

59
00:06:43.811 --> 00:06:49.570
While many animals migrate at least once in their lives, they do not always survive.

60
00:06:49.571 --> 00:06:54.011
Migration is full of hazards, both natural and human made.

61
00:06:54.012 --> 00:07:02.401
Natural hazards include changes in the weather, scarcity of food, drought, predators, and the physical demands of the long journey.

62
00:07:02.402 --> 00:07:06.667
Human constructions can also be hazardous to migratory animals.

63
00:07:06.668 --> 00:07:14.064
Skyscrapers and radio towers block the paths of migrating birds, while dams block the rivers that fish travel.

64
00:07:14.065 --> 00:07:20.396
Through their research, scientists are learning how people can minimize their interference with migratory routes.

65
00:07:20.397 --> 00:07:27.094
There are still many aspects of migration that scientists do not understand and mysteries to be revealed.

66
00:07:27.095 --> 00:07:35.143
But that won't stop millions of animals from making their amazing journeys─just as they have been doing for thousands of years.

